# Operator decision on PR-002 (goal-status exposure)

The reviewer is **correct**, and this aligns with the brief's own Risks note
("read the SAME per-task result map the scheduler already produces — don't
re-derive task status from disk"). The previous plan exposed goal-status only via a
standalone `_compute_goal_status`, with `_run_pipeline` **re-loading `goal.json`**
after `process_batch` returns — that re-load IS the disk-re-derivation drift the
brief forbids, and it makes the status "available only if a caller independently
re-loads the manifest," which is the scope change PR-002 names. Do NOT proceed on
the helper-only design.

Re-plan with this concrete, mandatory API (this closes PR-002 — implement exactly):

1. **`_run_batch(batch_dir) -> tuple[dict[str, str], GoalStatus | None]`** — the real
   batch worker. Load `goal.json` **once**, run the layered scheduler, and compute the
   goal-status from the **in-memory** `(results, deps)` it already holds — never
   re-read `goal.json` or any task state from disk. Flat mode (no manifest) → second
   element is `None`.
2. **`process_batch(batch_dir) -> dict[str, str]`** stays a thin backward-compatible
   wrapper: `return _run_batch(batch_dir)[0]`. Every existing caller and all Slice A
   tests (`results == {...}` assertions) remain byte-for-byte valid — the
   `dict[str, str]` contract is preserved.
3. **`_run_pipeline` calls `_run_batch` directly** to obtain BOTH results and
   goal-status in a single pass. **Delete the planned `_run_pipeline` re-load of
   `goal.json`** entirely (and drop the corresponding "re-loads goal.json" Risk — it no
   longer applies). The `GOAL COMPLETE` / `GOAL INCOMPLETE` line is rendered from the
   returned `GoalStatus`.
4. **`_compute_goal_status(results, manifest_task_ids) -> GoalStatus`** remains a pure,
   unit-testable helper called inside `_run_batch` (no I/O, no subprocess). `GoalStatus`
   carries `complete` / `done_count` / `total` / `blocked_ids` (blocked_ids in
   `_topo_layers` dispatch order). The manifest task-id list is passed in from
   `_run_batch`'s in-memory `deps` — not reloaded.

This satisfies "available from `process_batch`'s run, unit-testable, not only printed"
(via `_run_batch`, which `process_batch` and `_run_pipeline` both go through), keeps
the public `dict[str, str]` contract, and eliminates the manifest double-read.

Keep the PR-001 / PR-003 / PR-004 resolutions exactly as already accepted (concrete
affected files; no goal-level line after a manifest-validation abort; unknown
`ceilings.*` = tolerate-and-ignore). Update outcome.md's Affected files (add
`_run_batch`), the goal-status section, and the Risks accordingly.

On the reviewer's "Uncertain" item: **the brief IS authoritative.** The umbrella design
doc (`docs/decisions/2026-06-27-goal-mode-design.md`) lives on the not-yet-merged PR
#110 branch, so its absence on this branch is expected; the brief faithfully encodes
the Slice C cut. The design doc is NOT required to land in this PR.

USER_DECISION: REVISE_PLAN
